        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Immobilized Cell Bioreactor (ICB) of the Water Recovery System (WRS) was the primary site of wastewater treatment for organic materials and the first step in the water treatment system. Before the test, the ICB was inoculated with a pre-mixed commercial inoculum of microorganisms. On startup, the inoculum was recirculated through the reactor for 72 hours, after which the reactor began treating real waste.
<P>
The ICB treated a mixed feed of water from the laundry, shower, and hand wash, as well as water from oral hygiene activities, urine and humidity condensate. The ICB operated in an upflow, co-current manner. Wastewater and air entered the bottom of the reactor, flowed up its length, and exited the reactor. Valves could be configured to either drain or pump the effluent to the Trickling Filter Bioreactor (TFB). The ICB was constructed primarily of acrylic, which allowed for observation of the microbial film and air and liquid flow patterns. The nominal influent flow rate was 75 ml/min. This feed was mixed with recycle from the TFB effluent tank which flowed at 150 ml/min. Air was fed to the system at 1.5 liters per minute. Based on feed tank inventory, the flow rate was adjusted twice a day at 7:00 am and 4:00 pm.
<P>
The main body of the reactor consisted of a 25.4 cm (10 in) cylindrical tube, 218 cm (86 in) in height. The tube was filled with acrylic plates spaced 2.54 cm (1 in) apart. The plates were held apart with spacer bolts. Each plate was covered on both sides by a thin sheet of porous polymer immobilization support. This arrangement resulted in a series of vertically stacked chambers lined with a porous polymer on the top and bottom surfaces. The microbial biofilm preferentially colonized on these porous supports.<END>
        </div>

